turbo run test at d668fd0: 24 tasks successful. @liberty/web 72 files / 1265 tests passed; @liberty/backend 19 tests; all workspace packages green.

THE REGRESSION gpt-architect ASKED FOR BY NAME, in playback-session-authentication.test.ts, written as the six claims that would each individually have let the defect back in rather than as one assertion about an outcome:

 - a VALID request -- not a malformed one, which the schema would refuse whatever the gate did, so it could not tell a fail-closed gate from an absent one -- is answered unavailable / authentication_not_configured;
 - 503 through the envelope, with no-store;
 - the resolver is NEVER CALLED, which is the clause "Do NOT continue into content/provider resolution" asserted as a mechanism rather than as a property of the response body;
 - request.bodyUsed is FALSE at the refusal, which is the verdict's "so the absence-of-auth case cannot become a content oracle either" -- practical because the gate sits in front of readJsonBody for every branch and not for the signed-out one only;
 - the refusal is BYTE-IDENTICAL across a real title, an invented title, a malformed id and a body that is not JSON;
 - and the two events under authentication_not_configured keep DIFFERENT details, because "configure an identity store" and "your identity store is down" send an operator to different places, while both details stay caller-invariant and content-invariant.

The old test that asserted the fall-through -- "decides exactly as it did before this gate existed" -- is gone rather than skipped. It was the defect written down as an expectation.

TWO TESTS ADDED FOR THE MISATTRIBUTION FOUND WHILE DOING THIS, in watch-session.test.ts: the not-configured panel now says WHICH configuration is missing, and a deployment with no identity system is never mapped to signed-out -- there is no sign-in that could complete there, so offering one would be the dead end this task removes, wearing the label of the fix.

Lint and build recorded with the typecheck gate; this gate is the unit suite.

PASS against 061df207a6648919c7c3372f8b67640c11eb3434, transcribed from gpt-architect's round-94 verdict. gpt-architect cannot execute this command in this environment, so --transcribed-by names who typed it; the verdict is gpt-architect's and the wording below is theirs where quoted.

"The Round-93 corrective resolves the blocking security defect."

THE ACCEPTED SEMANTICS, as given:
 - authenticated caller -> normal playback decision;
 - configured identity system + no valid session -> unauthenticated / 401 / not_authenticated;
 - identity system exists but cannot answer -> unavailable / 503;
 - deployment has no identity system configured -> unavailable / 503 / authentication_not_configured;
 - no unidentified deployment caller reaches content/provider resolution;
 - authentication refusal occurs before request-body parsing;
 - missing-auth responses therefore cannot vary with content id;
 - explicit development identity behavior remains separate and continues to work.

THE NEW REASON CODE IS APPROVED, and the ruling goes further than accepting it -- it forbids the alternative I had been weighing. "authentication_not_configured is APPROVED as the reason code for the no-identity-system case. Do not reuse provider_not_configured; that identifies the wrong subsystem and sends the operator to the wrong remedy."

FOUR REMEDIES, TO BE KEPT SEPARATE, in gpt-architect's own enumeration:
 - not_authenticated -> viewer remedy -> sign in.
 - authentication_not_configured -> operator remedy -> configure identity.
 - the identity-store failure / provider_unavailable-style unavailable branch -> operator/runtime remedy -> restore dependency.
 - provider_not_configured -> operator remedy -> configure playback provider.

WHY THE REGRESSION WAS ACCEPTED, and it is the list of properties rather than the fact of a passing test: "valid request is refused in a deployment with no auth system; 503 is preserved through the shared envelope; provider/content resolver is never called; request body is still unread at refusal; bodies are byte-identical across content variations."

THE E2E IDENTITY HARNESS IS SEPARATELY APPROVED, with its properties enumerated: the pinned PostgreSQL 16.10 service; the committed migration applied directly with psql; CI asserting all eight expected tables exist; the account created through the application's own sign-up route; the session cookie issued by the real auth system; the harness neither minting sessions nor mutating verification state in PostgreSQL directly; one account and session per Playwright worker; bounded handling of real 429 rate limiting accepted and explicitly "not a test retry"; and development mode remaining database-free so the development identity mechanism continues to be exercised.

THE ONE CONFIGURATION I ASKED TO BE RULED ON IS ACCEPTED, WITH A BOUNDARY. Disabling email verification is accepted "only for the production E2E identity axis", because "that harness is testing authentication/session behavior, not verification delivery, and the two alternatives would be worse: fake/expose a verification URL; mutate application-owned verification state directly in PostgreSQL." And then the boundary, which is now a standing constraint on anyone who touches the composition root: "Do not generalize that setting to production defaults."

npx playwright test --project=chromium --project=api, development mode: 103 passed / 14 skipped, 0 failed, 1.9m -- up from 93/14, the ten new tests being player-controls.spec.ts. Run again after this round's changes to the control bar, not only before them.

THE TEN, AND WHY EACH IS HERE RATHER THAN IN A UNIT TEST. apps/web has no DOM, so a keyboard clause tested without a keyboard is not tested:
  - every control the acceptance names is on the page and has an accessible name;
  - THE NATIVE VIDEO CHROME IS GONE -- two seek bars that disagree about the position is worse than one;
  - the whole bar is reachable with TAB, in order, with nothing skipped;
  - SPACE operates the player AND DOES NOT SCROLL THE PAGE;
  - the arrow keys move the volume rather than the page;
  - M mutes and the button says which state it is in;
  - a track menu opens, announces itself, and says so when there is nothing to choose;
  - ESCAPE closes the menu;
  - THE BAR NEVER LEAVES THE ACCESSIBILITY TREE, however it is dimmed -- which is the acceptance clause "an idle-hide overlay that never hides a control from a keyboard or screen-reader user", and is why the idle state is opacity rather than display:none, visibility:hidden, aria-hidden or inert, each of which would remove the controls from the tree or the keyboard;
  - the seek bar announces a TIME rather than a percentage.

WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT ASSERTED, AND THE REASON MATTERS MORE THAN THE OMISSION: THAT THE PICTURE MOVES. The demo catalog's sources are fixtures and no real media decodes in this harness, so a test that clicked Play and waited for `playing` would be asserting the fixture rather than the control. What a command reaching the adapter looks like is covered in the adapter's own suite; what a viewer can OPERATE is covered here.

NOT COVERED BY ANY SUITE, AND STATED RATHER THAN LEFT TO BE FOUND: the re-application of a viewer's track choice across a real candidate switch, end to end in a browser. The DECISION has 11 unit tests and four caught mutations, and the wiring that issues it is three lines in one event branch -- but no harness in this repository produces two candidates with real track lists, because no real media decodes here. It is honest unit coverage of the rule plus unverified wiring of the issue, and it is the first thing to exercise when a real stream exists.

